# Commit Conventions

## When to Commit

Standing instruction from the project owner: work proceeds **step by step**, and every verified step is
committed and pushed to GitHub (`git push`) immediately — one logical unit of work per commit.

- Commit only after the quality gate passes for that step; never commit a broken build.
- Push right after committing so the remote always mirrors verified progress.
- Never force-push, rewrite published history, or skip hooks (`--no-verify`).
- If no remote is configured yet, commit locally and report that the push is pending.

## Commit Message Format

Single-line only. No body.

```
<type>(<scope>): <short summary>
```

- Max 72 characters total
- Summary is lowercase, no period at the end
- **Never add a multi-line body** — if the subject line is clear, nothing else is needed

### Types

| Type | When to use |
| --- | --- |
| `feat` | New feature or capability |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `refactor` | Code change with no behavior change |
| `chore` | Config, deps, tooling, non-source changes |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `test` | Adding or updating tests |
| `style` | Formatting only (no logic change) |

### Scope

Use the crate name without the `wp-` prefix, or the area: `hooks`, `query`, `db`, `auth`, `php-bridge`,
`php-vm`, `server`, `oracle`, `contracts`, `lints`, `ci`, `adr`, etc.
For a sub-area use `crate/module` e.g. `hooks/dispatch`, `db/prepare`.

### Examples

```
feat(hooks): add priority-ordered filter dispatch
feat(hooks/dispatch): support callback removal during iteration
fix(db/prepare): match wpdb placeholder escaping for %i identifiers
refactor(query): extract query var parsing into pure planner
test(php-bridge): add by-reference callback identity contract tests
chore(lints): deny unwrap_used across the workspace
docs(adr): record embedded vs worker VM decision
```

## Granularity Rules

Each commit must be **independently revertable**:

- One feature → one commit (or a few if logically separable)
- Schema change → separate commit from the feature using it
- Refactor → never bundled with a feature or fix
- Config/tooling changes → always their own commit

```
// ✅ GOOD — revertable in isolation
chore(lints): add workspace clippy restriction lints
feat(options): add autoloaded option loading
refactor(content): move post SQL into storage adapter

// ❌ BAD — impossible to revert one thing without the other
feat: add options api, fix hook order bug, update lints
```

## Commit Checklist

Before committing, verify:

- [ ] `cargo fmt --all --check` passes
- [ ] `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` passes
- [ ] `cargo test --workspace --all-features` passes and coverage gates hold (`rust-testing.mdc`)
- [ ] No upstream WordPress/PHP code, comments, assets or tests committed (`clean-room-copyright.mdc`)
- [ ] No `println!`/`dbg!`/`unwrap()`/`todo!()`/`unimplemented!()` in production code
- [ ] No new `unsafe` outside ADR-approved crates; every block has a `// SAFETY:` comment
- [ ] No new dependency without approval (see `rust-dependencies-tooling.mdc`)
- [ ] No commented-out code committed
- [ ] Commit message matches the type and scope of the actual change
